\p “I'm¦124755 your¦124759 servant¦124762 Ruth¦124756,” she¦124754 replied¦124749, “Spread¦124758 your cloak¦124759 over¦124760 me\f + \fr 3:9 \ft This was the cultural way for Ruth to request Boaz to take her as his wife.\f* because¦124763 you¦124765 have a responsibility as our close relative.”
\p
\v 10 “My dear girl, may Yahweh¦124770 bless¦124768 you.” Boaz replied¦124767, “You¦124772 have¦124772 shown more¦124775 kindness¦124773 now that before,\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Probably referring to Ruth's faithfulness to her mother-in-law.\f* because you haven't chased after¦124781 the¦124774 young¦124782 men¦124782, whether¦124783 rich¦124788 or poor¦124785.
\v 11 So¦124790 now¦124790 dear girl¦124809, don't¦124792 be afraid¦124794. I'll¦124799 do¦124792 what you've asked¦124798 for¦124801, because¦124802 the¦124806 whole¦124795 town knows¦124803 that¦124808 you're¦124798 an honourable woman¦124809.
\v 12 It¦124825 is¦124822 true that I¦124820 have a responsibility towards your family,\x + \xo 3:12: \xt Ruth 2:20.\x* but¦124813 there's¦124822 another man who's more¦124825 closely related \add to you all\add* than¦124825 me.
\v 13 Stay¦124827 here for the rest¦124847 of the night¦124829, and¦124830 then in¦124831 the morning¦124831, if¦124832 he'll¦124834 accept then he can \add marry\add* you. But¦124830 if¦124837 he's¦124840 not¦124839 willing¦124840 \add to take care of you\add*, then as Yahweh¦124846 lives, I'll¦124842 do it¦124830 myself. Now lie¦124847 down¦124847 here until¦124848 morning¦124850.”
\p
\v 14 So¦124852 \add Ruth\add* lay¦124852 \add there\add* near his¦124853 feet¦124853 until¦124859 morning¦124861, but¦124852 she¦124876 got up before¦124863 it¦124862 was¦124873 light enough to be¦124873 recognised¦124865 as Boaz had said¦124870, “\add It's best\add* if¦124874 no one knows¦124873 that¦124874 a woman¦124877 visited the¦124877 threshing¦124878 floor¦124878.”
\v 15 Then Boaz said¦124880, “Bring your cloak¦124882 here and¦124886 hold¦124886 it¦124888 out¦124891.” So¦124880 she¦124889 held¦124889 it¦124890 while he¦124891 poured a generous amount of barley¦124894 into it, and lifted it onto her \add shoulder\add*. Then \add ?he or she\add*\f + \fr 3:15 \ft TC: There's no separate Hebrew word for ‘he’ or ‘she’ here—it's part of the verb ‘he/she_went’ but it's unclear if this original verb was marked \+it masculine\+it* or \+it feminine\+it*, hence you'll find both alternatives in various Bible translations.\f* went¦124897 into the¦124898 town¦124898.
\v 16 When¦124900 Ruth got home, her mother-in-law¦124903 asked¦124904 her, “My¦124908 daughter¦124908, \add how did everything go\add*?”
