\p
\v 18 “\add Okay\add* my¦124941 daughter¦124941,” Naomi responded¦124939. “Wait \add now\add* until¦124942 we see¦124944 what happens, because¦124948 that¦124943 man¦124951 won't¦124949 rest today until the¦124951 matter¦124947 gets settled.”
\c 4
\s1 Boaz takes over the land and gets Ruth as a wife
\rem /s1 Boaz Marries Ruth; The Marriage of Boaz and Ruth
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile,\add* Boaz¦124960 went¦124961 to \add the place inside\add* the¦124962 town gate¦124962 \add (where people conducted their official business)\add* and¦124963 sat¦124963 down¦124963 there¦124964. Just as he¦124969 had hoped, the man responsible for Naomi's family came along—the man whom¦124968 Boaz had mentioned earlier—and Boaz called out to him by¦124967 name and¦124972 said¦124972, “Come over here¦124976 and sit¦124974 down.” So¦124960 the¦124966 man came over and sat¦124980 down¦124980.
\v 2 Then¦124982 Boaz called over ten¦124983 of the older, well-respected men¦124984 from the town¦124986 and¦124987 asked¦124987 them to sit¦124988 \add and witness their discussion\add*, so they¦124991 sat¦124991 down¦124991 \add with them\add*.
\v 3 Then¦124993 Boaz asked¦124993 the¦124995 other relative, “The¦124996 part¦124995 of¦124998 the countryside¦124996 that¦124997 belonged¦124998 to¦124994 our¦124998 relative Elimelek¦124999 is for sale. Naomi¦125001, who¦125002 \add recently\add* returned¦125002 from¦125003 Moab¦125004, is selling¦125000 it¦124993.
\v 4 I thought that¦125027 I should¦125027 tell¦125025 you \add about it\add* and \add in case\add* you buy \add the land\add* while the people sitting¦125013 here are listening, including¦125014 these leaders of¦125016 our people. If¦125017 you¦125019 want to¦125026 buy it back into the family, then do so¦125027. But¦125006 if¦125029 you do not¦125030 want to buy it back, then let me¦125026 know¦125027, because¦125029 you¦125031 are the closest relative to¦125032 Elimelek, and¦125033 I am¦125034 next after¦125034 you.”
\p “I'll¦125008 buy it¦125035,” the¦125015 man replied¦125007.
\p
\v 5 “When¦125039 you buy the¦125044 land from¦125045 Naomi¦125046,” Boaz¦125040 continued¦125039, “you will also¦125047 need to¦125055 marry Ruth¦125048, our deceased relative's widow from Moab, so that she'll have¦125053 a son who will inherit¦125061 the property and¦125039 continue Elimelek's family line.”
\p
\v 6 “I can't buy it¦125063 back myself, then¦125063,” the closer relative answered¦125063. “If¦125084 I did that, I would ruin¦125073 my¦125076 own¦125076 son's inheritance¦125076. You¦125080 can have the land and¦125063 the¦125064 woman instead of¦125076 me¦125070, because¦125084 I \add simply\add* can't take it.”
\p
\v 7 (At that¦125090 time¦125091 in Yisrael¦125092, people had a different way of buying \add land\add* or exchanging¦125098 \add goods between them\add*. In¦125092 order to¦125099 make these matters¦125102 legally binding, one man would take off¦125103 one of¦125105 his¦125105 sandals¦125105 and¦125106 give¦125106 it¦125106 to the other¦125107 party. That was¦125109 the way people in Yisrael confirmed¦125099 what they had agreed to do.)\x + \xo 4:7-8: \xt Deu 25:9.\x*
\p
\v 8 “You buy it¦125112 yourself,” the¦125113 relative said¦125112 and¦125118 he¦125118 took off¦125118 one of¦125119 his¦125119 sandals¦125119 \add and gave it to Boaz\add*.
\v 9 Then¦125121 Boaz¦125122 said¦125121 to¦125123 the¦125126 respected men¦125126 and to all the other people¦125126 who were¦125127 there, “Today you¦125128 have all witnessed¦125127 that I've bought from¦125144 Naomi¦125145 all the property that belonged¦125142 to Elimelek¦125137, Kilion¦125142, and¦125143 Mahlon¦125143.
\v 10 I¦125154 am also¦125147 taking Ruth¦125150, Mahlon's¦125153 widow from¦125170 Moab, to be my wife¦125152 so that she can have a¦125156 son. Everyone will consider that son to¦125157 be Elimelek's descendant and he will inherit¦125163 the¦125158 property and carry on Elimelek's family name¦125158 among¦125170 his relatives¦125171 and here in his hometown.\x + \xo 4:10: \xt Deu 25:5-6.\x* You've seen and heard that today \add and can tell anyone who asks about it\add*.”
\p
\v 11 All¦125179 the¦125181 respected men¦125181, and the others who were¦125184 sitting at¦125184 the town gate¦125184, agreed, and they¦125178 said¦125178, “Yes, \add we are your\add*. We¦125186 pray that¦125182 Yahweh¦125188 will allow this woman¦125191 who¦125192 will be¦125192 coming¦125192 into¦125193 your¦125195 home, to be like¦125196 Rahel¦125196 \add !(Rachel¦125196)\add* and¦125198 Leah¦125198, the¦125201 two¦125201 who gave birth to our ancestors and started our people¦125181, Yisrael¦125205.\x + \xo 4:11: \xt Gen 29:31.\x* May¦125187 you become great in the clan of¦125204 Efratah¦125209 and¦125210 famous here in¦125213 Beyt-Lehem¦125214!
\v 12 We pray that your family will be¦125216 like¦125218 the¦125218 family of¦125218 your ancestor Perez¦125219, son of Judah and Tamar¦125223, because of the¦125227 many descendants¦125227 that¦125228 Yahweh¦125230 will¦125229 give¦125229 to¦125231 you and this¦125235 young¦125234 woman¦125234.”\x + \xo 4:12: \xt Gen 38:27-30.\x*
\s1 Boaz' descendants
\rem /s1 The Genealogy of David; The Descendants of Boaz; Boaz and His Descendants
\p
\v 13 So¦125237 Boaz¦125238 married Ruth¦125241, and \add when\add* he¦125246 \add ≈slept with\add* her¦125247, Yahweh¦125249 enabled her to become¦125242 pregnant and she¦125252 gave¦125252 birth¦125252 to a son¦125253.
\v 14 The¦125256 women¦125256 \add in Beyt-Lehem\add* said¦125255 to¦125257 Naomi¦125259, “Praise Yahweh¦125261 for¦125265 giving you a¦125266 \add male descendant\add* to preserve your family today. \add We pray to Yahweh that people throughout\add* Yisrael¦125270 will greatly respect him.
\v 15 Your¦125279 daughter-in-law¦125281, who¦125282 loves¦125284 you and who's¦125286 been¦125289 better¦125289 to¦125290 you than¦125291 if¦125280 you had¦125285 seven¦125291 sons¦125292, has¦125285 given¦125285 birth¦125285 to¦125285 him¦125285. Now he'll make you feel young¦125292 again and he'll take care of¦125279 you when you get old¦125279.”
\v 16 Then¦125294 Naomi¦125295 picked¦125294 up the¦125298 baby¦125298 and¦125299 held him¦125299 closely and¦125301 became¦125301 like second mother to¦125303 him¦125303.
\p
\v 17 The¦125308 women who were living nearby said¦125310, “It¦125307 is¦125311 as if Naomi¦125314 now has¦125311 a son¦125313!” They¦125306 named him¦125307 Obed¦125317.
\rem /s1 The ancestry of David
\p \add Eventually\add* Obed went on to¦125310 become the¦125319 father¦125319 of¦125319 Yishay¦125321 \add !(Jesse¦125321)\add*, who became the¦125322 father¦125322 of¦125322 \add King\add* David¦125323.
\v 18 Here is¦125327 a list of the¦125327 descendants¦125327 of¦125327 Perez¦125329: Perez's¦125328 son was¦125327 Hezron¦125333.
\v 19 Hezron's son was Ram¦125340 and¦125340 Ram's¦125339 son was Amminadab¦125344.
\v 20 Amminadab's¦125346 son was Nahshon¦125351 and¦125351 Nahshon's¦125350 son was Salmon.
\v 21 Salmon's¦125357 son was Boaz¦125362 and¦125362 Boaz's¦125361 son was Obed¦125366.
\v 22 Obed's son was Yishay¦125373 \add !(Jesse¦125373)\add* and¦125373 Yishay's¦125372 son was King David¦125377.
